<h2 linkable>Gaining Access</h2>
<p>
    This is where the fun begins. Getting boards out of the CRT was pretty nerve-wracking as I've never really worked
    around any voltages higher than US Residential AC before. It was pretty neat how Sony designed all these boards
    to elegantly slide out of the rear. Here I got a glimpse at a crack on the A board PCB - more on that later.
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03097.jpg"
         alt="Back side of the television with the cover removed, exposing the electronic components within.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03098.jpg" alt="Close-up of the UX board on the television.">
    <img scaled basis="1/3" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03101.jpg"
         alt="Close-up of the internals of the television.">
</gallery>
<p>
    I tried to wiggle things around as much as I could but ultimately there was no avoiding having to deal with the
    anode. I got some alligator clips to ground a screwdriver and poked around under the little suction cup to discharge
    the system. If it makes a pop sound, you know you did it right. If it doesn't, then either it was already discharged
    or you did it wrong and the back of this TV could be the last thing you ever see. My TV did not pop. I did not die.
    Glorious success.
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03110.jpg"
         alt="Gloved hand apprehensively holding a screwdriver in front of the back of the TV.">
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03111.jpg" alt="Backside of the tube with anode removed.">
</gallery>

<p>
    I was able to get the A board all the way out now. It was pretty nasty, so I stuck a shop fan next to it and gave it
    a quick brushing to get most of the dust off. I also got a closer look at the extent of the damage to the PCB (that
    would be why my tuner and front I/O aren't working). It's right at the end of where the PCB loses structural support
    from the plastic frame of the TV, so this is probably the aftermath of a drop.
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/IMG_4993.HEIC"
         alt="Main board getting the dust brushed off of it.">
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/IMG_4991.HEIC" alt="Close up of the crack in the PCB.">
</gallery>

<h2 linkable>A Minor Diversion</h2>
<p>
    I spent several hours running wires to replace the severed traces from the crack. I would have been totally
    overwhelmed by this if it weren't for the service manual telling me exactly what each pin on the nearby connectors
    needed to go to. I probed around with a multimeter to confirm connectivity and replace it where needed. Sadly, I
    haven't had any luck getting the tuner to work but the front A/V connectors are back in action. I suspect I did
    something wrong connecting the I<sup>2</sup>C lines for the tuner and the TV is not able to get it to actually
    change channels or something along those lines.
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03113.jpg"
         alt="A board with wires to repair damaged traces in place.">
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03114.jpg" alt="Closer picture of affected area on board.">
</gallery>
<h2 linkable>Actually Installing the Mod</h2>
<p>
    Alright, with that out of the way, I finally proceeded to doing the actual mod! Since my model
    varied from the directions I was following from Sunthar's site, it took a bit of staring at schematics to find the
    right spots for everything.
</p>
<p>
    First, let's start with the connections on the A board. There is a group of resistors east of the tuning chip
    to be removed. The not-connected-to-ground side of these pads are your attachment points for the red (R1123),
    green (R1128), and blue (R1133) signal wires from the RGB SCART board. It wasn't explicitly said anywhere in the
    instructions, but the colors on the diagrams correspond to the colors on the ribbon cables provided with the kit.
    The
    right side of the nearby R1353 through-hole resistor is the connection point for the blanking interval wire.
</p>
<img src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/diagram-rgbb.svg" loading="lazy" class="diagram"
     alt="Diagram of where to connect red, green, blue, and blanking wires.">
<p>
    And now the connections on the UX board. You need to sever the connection on the little island marked for the right
    audio input in order for stereo sound to work. You can see in the photograph where I cut this line on input 3 before
    realizing I needed to use an input with S-Video. Audio lines for the two channels go to the center plug on each of
    those. S-Video detect goes to pin 6, ground on pin 2 (or anywhere really), and sync on pin 4 (the luminance input).
</p>
<img src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/diagram-sync.svg" loading="lazy" class="diagram"
     alt="Diagram showing where to connect the S-Video detect, sync-on-luma, ground, and audio wires.">

<p>
    I hijacked my Video 1 input for this, but if I were to do this again, I might elect to tap into Video 2 instead,
    especially since I hardly ever use the front inputs on the TV. That would also keep all of the wires contained to a
    single board. Below I have a diagram of how I believe you would wire that up. You'd still need to pull the front
    I/O board to sever the stereo audio connection.
</p>
<img src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/diagram-front.svg" loading="lazy" class="diagram"
     alt="Diagram showing how one would connect the RGB mod to Video 2 (Front Panel)">

<p>
    Other odds and ends, another diode that was included with the kit was added inline with R067 on the top side of the
    A board. That's everything wired up!
</p>
<gallery>
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/IMG_5003.HEIC"
         alt="Diode added to top side of A board.">
    <img scaled basis="1/2" src="/images/2026-11-CrtRgb/DSC03122.jpg"
         alt="A and UX boards with wires connected.">
</gallery>

<h2 linkable>Things Go Awry</h2>
<p>
    And with that, I carefully reassembled the TV to test out the changes. I plugged in my Super Famicom and turned on
    the power to the TV. I heard it degauss... then, nothing. I was really hoping I didn't just break this thing; I did
    just solder a whole mess of wires on. I walked around the back of the TV and froze in terror. I had not seated the
    anode correctly, and it had popped out and was dangling around and fizzling away... <i>menacingly</i>. I don't know
    if I've ever felt so scared in my life, I still had the jitters for several hours after I finished working on the
    TV. If I had hair on my arms, it would have been standing on end. I didn't want to stick my hand close enough to
    the TV to put my little alligator clip on a ground point around the tube, so I went and got a set of jumper cables
    for a car and used that to discharge the set. There was certainly a pop this time. Forgive me for not taking any
    pictures during that part; I was a bit preoccupied.
</p>

<h2>Victory Lap</h2>
<p>
    After connecting the anode properly, I tried the TV again, and to my shock (ack!) it was working just fine. I was
    thinking I would have managed to short something out in my tinkering but the mod was working nicely. I learned that
    my SFC probably needs new capacitors but found the video to be much cleaner when I connected my PS3 or Dreamcast. My
    cat was there and she was clearly very impressed by it.
</p>
